-- USE CASE: one-shot escaping coroutine (second-class affine Suspension).
-- A producer runs to its first `Coro.suspend`, parking its continuation
-- below the effect boundary. `start` returns the FIRST outcome as a `Step`:
-- either `Completed r` (the producer finished) or `Suspended x g`, where `x`
-- is the yielded value and `g` is an opaque Suspension handle.
-- The Suspension is second-class via the carrier rule (can't be returned,
-- stored, or listed) and affine: `run`/`step`/`cancel` consume it exactly once.
-- The parked continuation lives in the runtime; re-establishing the Coro handler
-- on resume is done automatically by deep re-install.
--
-- Surface: plain functions, no `with coro in` needed.
--   `start producer` -- installs Coro, runs to first suspend, returns a `Step`
--   `case ... of`     -- dispatch on `Completed r` / `Suspended x g`
--   `step g v`        -- advance one step, returns the next `Step`
--   `run g v`         -- single-shot: resume once and assume completion
--   `cancel g`        -- discard a Suspension without resuming it
--
-- The Suspension is second-class and affine: a compile error if consumed twice,
-- returned from a function, put in a list, or stored in a constructor.
module Main
import Base
import Control

-- Producer: suspends with 42; when resumed with v, returns v + 1.
producer : () -> U64 with Coro U64 U64
producer u = (Coro.suspend 42) + 1

-- Driver: runs the producer to its first suspension, then resumes it with
-- x * 10 (= 420). The producer finishes as 420 + 1 = 421.
main : U64
main = case start producer of
  Completed r   -> r
  Suspended x g -> run g (x * 10)
